  "summary": "Recent preprints apply language-model embeddings to brain cell-type protein networks, MS2 spectra, and ontology entity normalization.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of learned language-model representations to map heterogeneous biological inputs onto functional or ontological structure. SEMPER aligns protein language model embeddings with cell-type-specific protein interaction networks across 442 adult human brain cell types and is designed for zero-shot use. Spec2Function infers biological function directly from MS2 spectra using a dual-encoder contrastive model combined with retrieval-augmented generation. The third item addresses a complementary bottleneck: a modular workflow combining BM25, fuzzy and n-gram matching, and SapBERT retrieval with reranking normalizes entities across HPO, Cell Ontology, Uberon, and GO. What is new is the joint use of contrastive alignment and retrieval to connect molecular or textual features to biological meaning, rather than a single shared dataset or task.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
